\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


Matrix scaling seeks diagonal matrices $D_1,D_2$ with positive diagonal entries such that $D_1AD_2$ has prescribed row and column marginals, up to the requested $\ell_1$ error. Here $m$ denotes the number of nonzero input entries. For the dense query question, $A\in\mathbb R_{\geq0}^{n\times n}$ satisfies $\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}\leq1$, its row-sum vector is $r_i=\sum_jA_{ij}$, and the required estimate satisfies $\|\widehat r-r\|_1\leq\varepsilon$. The notation $\widetilde O$ suppresses logarithmic factors.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
Quantum query complexity depends on how entries can be accessed, while running time also includes output and arithmetic costs. The supplied question does not spell out its entry oracle or output representation; these are source-model choices that must be stated in an answer.

Source-record limitation: The quantum matrix-entry oracle and output representation are absent; these determine both the scaling runtime and row-sum query-complexity targets.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can a quantum algorithm improve the $\widetilde O(m^{3/2})$ high-precision running time for general matrix scaling while retaining polylogarithmic dependence on $1/\varepsilon$? For a dense nonnegative $n\times n$ matrix whose entries sum to at most $1$, what is the quantum query complexity of approximating its row-sum vector to $\ell_1$ error $\varepsilon$? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2021/49}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record partially solved; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Improve high-accuracy quantum matrix scaling and determine the cost of estimating all row sums.

